+\emph{Comparison with Nielsen.} Three results should be distinguished.
+\begin{center}
+\small
+\begin{tabular}{p{0.17\textwidth}p{0.42\textwidth}p{0.32\textwidth}}
+\hline
+Result & Assumptions and method & Conclusion\\\hline
+Proposition~\ref{prop:probabilism} & (CC), (SA) and (N); selection theorem & every incoherent model is dominated by a probability\\
+Corollary~\ref{cor:probabilism} & quasi-strict propriety; lower semicontinuity of each $q\mapsto\ell(q,\omega)$ on $\Delta$ & the same, and every probability is undominated\\
+\cite[Theorems~1--2]{Nielsen2023} & quasi-strict propriety; continuity on $\Delta$ of $q\mapsto E_p\ell(q,\cdot)$ for each $p\in\Delta$; boundedness of each $\ell(p,\cdot)$, $p\in\Delta$ & each incoherent model is strictly improved upon at \emph{every} world by some probability; every probability is undominated\\\hline
+\end{tabular}
+\end{center}
+Corollary~\ref{cor:probabilism} fits beneath Proposition~\ref{prop:probabilism}: quasi-strict propriety supplies (SA) and (N), lower semicontinuity supplies (CC), and the perturbation argument supplies protection. Nielsen's continuity condition with $p=\delta_\omega$ makes each $q\mapsto\ell(q,\omega)$ continuous on $\Delta$, so his hypotheses imply those of Corollary~\ref{cor:probabilism}. Thus Proposition~\ref{prop:probabilism} isolates qualitative conditions sufficient for dominance, without numerical representation or comparisons of expected loss, and Corollary~\ref{cor:probabilism} needs weaker regularity than Nielsen's result. Neither recovers Nielsen's stronger conclusion of strict improvement at every world. Proposition~\ref{prop:probabilism} does not by itself establish protection; coherent admissibility supplies that separately. Truth-directedness belongs to our accuracy interpretation; it is not among Nielsen's admissibility conditions.
+
+\begin{remark}[Numerical representation]\label{rem:numerical-TD}
+Let $\Omega$ be infinite and $\omega\in\Omega$. No function $\ell(\cdot,\omega):\M\to[0,\infty]$ satisfies \ref{TD1} and \ref{TD2} with $m\preceq_\omega n$ read as $\ell(m,\omega)\le\ell(n,\omega)$. Indeed, $2^\Omega$ has at least the cardinality of $\mathbb R$; fix an injection $r\mapsto E_r$ from $(0,1)$ into $2^\Omega$. For $r\in(0,1)$ choose $a_r,b_r\in\M$ with errors in $\{0,1\}$: $e_\omega(a_r,E)=0$ iff $E=E_t$ for some $t<r$, and $e_\omega(b_r,E)=0$ iff $E=E_t$ for some $t\le r$. By \ref{TD2}, $\ell(b_r,\omega)<\ell(a_r,\omega)$; by \ref{TD1}, $\ell(a_s,\omega)\le\ell(b_r,\omega)$ whenever $r<s$. These values are finite: $\ell(b_r,\omega)<\ell(a_r,\omega)$, and $\ell(a_r,\omega)\le\ell(b_t,\omega)$ for $t<r$. So the intervals $(\ell(b_r,\omega),\ell(a_r,\omega))$, $r\in(0,1)$, are nonempty, open and pairwise disjoint, which is impossible in $\mathbb R$.
+
+So a real-valued loss can be truth-directed only in a weakened sense. One weakening keeps \ref{TD1} and requires strict improvement only when every proposition improves: $\ell(m,\omega)<\ell(n,\omega)$ whenever $e_\omega(m,E)<e_\omega(n,E)$ for every $E$. A weighted Brier loss $\ell(m,\omega)=\sum_kw_k\,e_\omega(m,E_k)^2$ on a countable family $(E_k)$ of events, with $w_k>0$ and $\sum_kw_k<\infty$, has this property. The example shows only that the weakened constraint has a real-valued representation; it does not satisfy the whole package above. For instance, it violates (N): since $2^\Omega$ is uncountable, some $E\notin\{E_k\}$, and changing a probability's value at $E$ alone gives an incoherent model (its values at $E$ and $\Omega\setminus E$ no longer sum to $1$) tied with it at every world. Requiring strict improvement only under uniform proportional improvement is another option.
+\end{remark}
+\end{chgblockb}
